WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.250
Clean toilets, healthy children and waste that stays out of the yard.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.810
This is the video for module 3 of Home Hygiene and Sanitation: Toilets, Children and Waste.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.010
A toilet protects your family only when faeces stay inside it.

4
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:25.470
That means the bowl or slab is clean, the hole is covered, flies cannot get in and out, and nothing leaks into drains or wells.

5
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.420
A dirty toilet pushes people, especially children, to go outside, which brings germs back into the yard.

6
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:41.800
Sanitary pads, nappies and plastic block pipes and fill pits fast. Wrap them and put them in a covered bin.

7
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:49.280
Put on rubber gloves and use the toilet-only cloth and brush. These tools never go into the kitchen.

8
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.620
Open the window or door for air.

9
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:59.520
Start with the cleanest parts: the door handle and light switch, then the flush or bucket handle.

10
00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.820
Then the seat top and lid, and the outside of the bowl.

11
00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:13.260
The inside of the bowl is last. Scrub it with a toilet brush and detergent. For a pit latrine, scrub the slab and footrests.

12
00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:26.010
Disinfect the seat, handle and slab with bleach diluted as its label says. Never mix it with toilet acid. Then wash your gloves, take them off, and wash your hands with soap.

13
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.480
Shared toilets usually fail because nobody is clearly responsible.

14
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:37.770
For example, six households in Ruth's compound share one toilet, and the soap always runs out.

15
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:45.160
At a compound meeting, she proposes that each household cleans one day a week, and they take turns on Sundays.

16
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:51.650
Then she lists the monthly supplies: detergent, bleach, soap, and gloves and a brush.

17
00:01:52.900 --> 00:02:04.190
That is about 7,800 naira a month, so each household pays about 1,300 naira into a kitty, kept by one person who records every payment.

18
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.610
A checklist pinned inside the door is ticked every day.

19
00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.800
These prices are approximate.

20
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:23.210
Many families believe a baby's faeces are clean. They are not. They carry the same germs as adult faeces, often more. Put them in the toilet, never the yard, a drain or the rubbish.

21
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.550
Empty a potty into the toilet, rinse it and clean it with soapy water.

22
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.220
Tip solids from cloth or disposable nappies into the toilet first.

23
00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.130
And wash your hands with soap after every change.

24
00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:49.820
Children learn by copying. For example, Grace in Jos teaches her son Tunde. First she washes her own hands slowly beside him, saying each step aloud.

25
00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:56.020
Then she guides his hands through each step, with a short song so he rubs long enough.

26
00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.400
Then he tries alone while she watches.

27
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:04.150
She praises him straight away: you washed your thumbs, well done.

28
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.910
She keeps a tick chart for 5 moments a day.

29
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.920
In week 1 he scores 22 out of 35, about 63%.

30
00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:18.260
He forgets most after playing outside, so she moves his stool and soap to the back door.

31
00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:23.170
In week 2 he scores 31 out of 35, about 89%.

32
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:31.080
Telling a child off makes hygiene a source of fear. Give a calm reminder, and praise the next correct wash.

33
00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:37.110
Rubbish feeds rats and flies, and old tins hold rainwater for mosquitoes.

34
00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.870
So sort at the source.

35
00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:45.060
For example, Esther in Lagos measured a week of her family's waste: 180 litres.

36
00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:53.560
By composting food waste and selling bottles and cans to a collector, only 60 litres needed collecting, a cut of two thirds.

37
00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:05.280
Never heap rubbish near the house or in drains. Do not burn plastics, because the smoke is harmful to children and neighbours. Use a registered collection service.

38
00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.540
To remember.

39
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.680
Keep the toilet clean and covered.

40
00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.970
Clean from the door handle to the inside of the bowl.

41
00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.710
Share toilet work with a rota and a kitty.

42
00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:20.310
Treat child faeces as dangerous, and teach by showing and praising.

43
00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.450
And sort your waste so less of it reaches the yard.

44
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:35.310
Now audit and clean your toilet and cost its supplies, run a two-week handwashing chart with a child, and do a one-week waste audit. Continue with the lessons below.
